<SiteLayout title="Talent, opportunity & athlete development — Bryan Kwan" description="An independent research manuscript exploring development environments and elite football and basketball representation." active="research">
  <section class="page-hero"><div class="wrap"><p class="eyebrow red">RESEARCH / JULY 1, 2026</p><h1>TALENT IS GLOBAL.<br>OPPORTUNITY IS LOCAL.</h1><p class="lede">How Development Environments Shape Athletic Greatness</p><div class="research-byline"><span>Bryan Kwan</span><span>Independent research manuscript</span></div><a class="button button-dark" style="margin-top:28px" href={href('talent-environment-research.pdf')} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read the manuscript (PDF) ↗</a></div></section>
  <div class="wrap case-layout"><nav class="chapter-nav" aria-label="Research chapters"><p>IN THIS STUDY</p><a href="#question">01 / The question</a><a href="#method">02 / The method</a><a href="#snapshot">03 / A view of the data</a><a href="#limits">04 / Interpretation</a></nav><article class="case-body">
    <section id="question"><h2>Where does opportunity<br>enter the picture?</h2><p>Elite athlete representation varies widely across countries. This exploratory study asks how those patterns relate to development environments, economic conditions, and population.</p><p>The aim is to build a reproducible comparison and expose the measurement choices behind it. The manuscript is independent research, not a peer-reviewed publication.</p></section>
    <section id="method"><h2>Build the comparison carefully.</h2><p>I assembled public datasets, validated country mappings, built country-level comparisons and exploratory regressions, and wrote the accompanying manuscript.</p><p>The workflow combines World Bank development indicators, HDI, active Transfermarkt player records, the NBA’s international opening-night roster, and a historical FIFA ranking snapshot. Different sources describe different moments and different definitions of representation.</p><div class="metric-grid"><div><strong>217</strong><span>Countries / territories in the validation report</span></div><div><strong>19,153</strong><span>Active 2025 football-player rows</span></div><div><strong>135</strong><span>International NBA roster rows</span></div></div><p class="resource-caption">Saved project validation figures; the measures come from different source populations.</p></section>
    <section id="snapshot"><h2>One snapshot of representation.</h2><figure><img src={href('media/nba-players-by-country.png')} alt="Country-level distribution of international players on 2025–26 NBA opening-night rosters" loading="lazy" width="1620" height="1260"/><figcaption>International players on 2025–26 NBA opening-night rosters, using the NBA’s country classifications. These are not birthplace or training-country measures.</figcaption></figure><p class="resource-caption">Source clarification: the original manuscript describes this sample as excluding U.S.-born players. The <a href="https://pr.nba.com/international-players-2025-26-nba-rosters/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">NBA’s published roster</a> instead uses its international-player classifications, which can include U.S.-born players. Interpret this figure as roster representation.</p></section>
    <section id="limits"><h2>A proxy is not the whole story.</h2><p>Country of citizenship is not the same as the place an athlete trained. Market values, roster membership, and national-team rankings are imperfect proxies for talent development. Small-country denominators and source coverage also affect comparisons.</p><p>The relationships are correlational. They should prompt more specific questions about systems and opportunity, rather than causal claims about what produces an elite athlete.</p><a class="button button-dark" href={href('talent-environment-research.pdf')} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read the methods & limitations ↗</a></section>
  </article></div>
</SiteLayout>
